const summary2 = table(['Idea', 'Rule'], [['Net GST of a dealer', r`$\text{output tax} - \text{input tax}$`], ['Same rate throughout', r`$\text{net GST} = r\% \text{ of (selling price} - \text{purchase price)}$`], ['Whole chain', 'Total to the government = GST on the final sale'], ['Inter-state link', 'IGST payable = output IGST − input CGST − input SGST'], ['Reverse', r`$\text{price} = \text{amount} \times \dfrac{100}{100 + r}$`]], "Summary table of part two: a dealer's net GST is output tax minus input tax; at one rate it is that rate of the dealer's margin; the total along a chain equals the GST on the final sale; on an inter-state link IGST payable is output IGST minus input CGST and SGST; in reverse problems the price is the amount times 100 over 100 plus r.", 'Part 2 on one card.');

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- deck 1
const d1 = deck({
  part: 1,
  title: 'Goods and Services Tax — Part 1: CGST, SGST, IGST and the bill',
  goals: ['Calculate the GST on an article at a given rate', 'Split the GST on an intra-state sale into equal CGST and SGST', 'Use IGST for a sale from one state to another', 'Find the amount a consumer pays after a discount and GST'],
  slides: [
    sl('title', 'Goods and Services Tax, Part 1: reading the bill', { b: 'Every bill you pay in a shop has a tax line. This chapter explains it.', v: billFlow, n: 'Welcome to the chapter on goods and services tax. Look at any shop bill in India and you will see a tax line under the price. In this part you will learn how that tax is calculated, why it is often split into two equal parts, and how a discount changes the bill. The picture shows the journey: price, plus tax, gives the amount, and the tax reaches the government.' }),
    sl('concept', 'What you will be able to do', { ul: ['Calculate **GST** on a price at a given rate', 'Split it into **CGST** and **SGST** for a sale within a state', 'Use **IGST** for a sale between states', 'Read a **tax invoice** with several items', 'Find the **amount paid** after a discount and GST'], n: 'Here are the goals for part one. You will calculate the tax on a price, split it into the central and state parts, and use the integrated tax when goods cross a state border. You will read a tax invoice with several items, and finally combine a discount with the tax to find what a customer really pays.' }),
    sl('concept', 'What is GST?', { b: 'The **Goods and Services Tax** is one tax on the supply of goods and services across India, in force since 1 July 2017. It replaced many separate taxes such as excise duty, VAT and service tax.', ul: ['Charged on **every sale** in a chain, from maker to shop to customer', 'Charged on **services** too: repairs, catering, hall rent, software', 'Paid by the **buyer**, collected by the **seller**, passed to the **government**'], v: chainFlow, n: 'The goods and services tax is a single tax on the supply of goods and services, in force across India since the first of July, twenty seventeen. It replaced a tangle of older taxes. It is charged at every sale, from the maker to the wholesaler, the retailer and the customer, and on services such as repairs and catering. The buyer pays it, the seller collects it, and the government receives it.' }),
    sl('concept', 'Rates of GST', { b: 'Each item has a rate fixed by the **GST Council** (the Union and state finance ministers together). Rates are revised from time to time, so **every problem states the rate to use**. This chapter uses 5%, 12%, 18% and 28%.', v: rateTable, n: 'Each kind of goods or service has its own rate, fixed by the council of the Union and state finance ministers, and these rates are revised from time to time. So never try to remember the rate for an item: every problem tells you the rate to use. In this chapter you will meet five, twelve, eighteen and twenty eight percent. The table also shows the halves we are about to meet.' }),
    sl('diagram', 'Within a state or across states?', { b: 'The **place of the seller and the buyer** decides how the tax appears on the bill.', v: headsTable, n: 'Here is the key distinction. If the seller and buyer are in the same state, the sale is intra-state, and the bill shows central tax and state tax, each at half the rate. If the goods go from one state to another, the sale is inter-state, and the bill shows integrated tax at the full rate. The rate itself does not change; only the heads of tax change.' }),
    sl('concept', 'The formulas', { m: [r`\text{GST} = \dfrac{r}{100} \times \text{price}`, r`\text{CGST} = \text{SGST} = \dfrac{r/2}{100} \times \text{price} \quad (\text{intra-state})`, r`\text{IGST} = \dfrac{r}{100} \times \text{price} \quad (\text{inter-state})`, r`\text{amount on the bill} = \text{price} + \text{GST}`], ul: ['The **price** here is the taxable value: the price after any discount, before tax'], v: billFlow, n: 'Three formulas do almost all the work. The tax is the rate over one hundred times the price. Within a state, the central and state parts are each half of it. Between states, the integrated tax is the whole of it. And the amount on the bill is the price plus the tax. Remember that the price means the value after any discount, but before tax.' }),
    sl('example', 'Worked example 1: a sale within Maharashtra', { b: `A furniture shop in Nagpur sells a study table for ${R(we1.price)} to a customer in Nagpur. GST is 12%. Find the CGST, the SGST and the amount of the bill.`, ul: [r`**Step 1 —** GST $= \dfrac{12}{100} \times 6400 = ${nm(we1.gst)}$`, r`**Step 2 —** Intra-state: CGST $= \text{SGST} = \dfrac{${nm(we1.gst)}}{2} = ${nm(we1.cgst)}$ (each at $6\%$)`, r`**Step 3 —** Amount $= 6400 + ${nm(we1.cgst)} + ${nm(we1.sgst)} = ${nm(we1.amount)}$`], v: we1Table, n: 'First find the full tax: twelve percent of six thousand four hundred is seven hundred and sixty eight rupees. Both people are in Nagpur, so the sale is within Maharashtra and the tax splits equally: three hundred and eighty four rupees of central tax and the same of state tax, each at six percent. Add both to the price. The bill is seven thousand one hundred and sixty eight rupees.' }),
    sl('check', 'Quick check: the central rate', { b: 'A geyser is sold within one city at a GST rate of 12%. At what rate is CGST charged?', chk: 'm02', n: 'Both buyer and seller are in the same city, so the sale is within one state. What happens to the twelve percent? Decide, then choose.' }),
    sl('example', 'Worked example 2: crossing a state border', { b: `A pump maker in Coimbatore (Tamil Nadu) sells a pump priced at ${R(we2i.price)} to a farmer in Palakkad (Kerala). GST is 18%. Write the tax on the bill. What if the farmer were in Tiruppur (Tamil Nadu)?`, ul: [r`Inter-state: IGST $= \dfrac{18}{100} \times 15500 = ${nm(we2i.igst)}$; amount ${R(we2i.amount)}`, r`Intra-state: CGST $= \text{SGST} = 9\% \text{ of } 15500 = ${nm(we2a.cgst)}$; amount ${R(we2a.amount)}`, 'The farmer pays the same either way'], v: we2Table, n: 'Coimbatore is in Tamil Nadu and Palakkad is in Kerala, so the pump crosses a state border and the whole eighteen percent is charged as integrated tax: two thousand seven hundred and ninety rupees. If the farmer were in Tiruppur, inside Tamil Nadu, the same amount would be split into one thousand three hundred and ninety five rupees of central tax and the same of state tax. The farmer pays eighteen thousand two hundred and ninety rupees either way.' }),
    sl('diagram', 'Reading a tax invoice', { b: 'A registered seller must give a **tax invoice**. These are the lines you use in sums.', v: invoiceParts, n: 'A registered seller gives a tax invoice. It shows the identification number of the seller, a description and a code for each item, the taxable value, the rate and amount of each tax, and the invoice value. In examination sums you mostly need the taxable value, the tax lines and the total, but it helps to know what the rest of the invoice means.' }),
    sl('example', 'Worked example 3: several items, several rates', { b: 'A stationery shop in Bhubaneswar bills a local student for the items below. Find the total GST, the CGST, the SGST and the invoice value.', ul: ['Tax **each line at its own rate**, then add', r`GST $= ${nm(we3.rows[0].gst)} + ${nm(we3.rows[1].gst)} + ${nm(we3.rows[2].gst)} = ${nm(we3.gst)}$`, r`CGST $= \text{SGST} = ${nm(we3.cgst)}$; invoice value $= ${nm(we3.price)} + ${nm(we3.gst)} = ${nm(we3.amount)}$`], v: we3Table, n: 'When the items have different rates, tax each line separately. Twelve percent of the bag is one hundred and forty four rupees, eighteen percent of the calculator is one hundred and fifty three, and five percent of the notebooks is twenty. The total tax is three hundred and seventeen rupees, split into one hundred and fifty eight rupees fifty paise each. The invoice value is two thousand seven hundred and sixty seven rupees.' }),
    sl('check', 'Quick check: equal halves', { b: 'True or false: on a sale within a state, central GST and state GST are each charged at half the GST rate.', chk: 'tf1', n: 'Think about the rate table you saw earlier. Is the split always equal, whatever the rate? Decide, and give a reason.' }),
    sl('concept', 'List price, discount and GST', { b: 'Shops often show a **list (marked) price** and offer a **discount**. GST is charged on the **selling price**, the price after the discount.', m: [r`\text{selling price} = \text{list price} - \text{discount}`, r`\text{amount paid} = \text{selling price} + \dfrac{r}{100} \times \text{selling price}`], v: discFlow, n: 'Many shops show a list price, also called the marked price, and offer a discount. The tax is not charged on the list price. It is charged on the selling price, the value after the discount. So the order is always the same: take off the discount first, then add the tax on what is left. The flow shows the steps.' }),
    sl('example', 'Worked example 4: discount, then GST', { b: `A refrigerator is listed at ${R(we4.list)}. A shop in Lucknow gives a discount of 15% and charges GST at 18% to a local customer. Find the CGST and the amount paid.`, ul: [r`**Step 1 —** Discount $= \dfrac{15}{100} \times 24000 = ${nm(we4.off)}$; selling price $= ${nm(we4.sp)}$`, r`**Step 2 —** GST $= \dfrac{18}{100} \times ${nm(we4.sp)} = ${nm(we4.gst)}$; CGST $= \text{SGST} = ${nm(we4.cgst)}$`, r`**Step 3 —** Amount $= ${nm(we4.sp)} + ${nm(we4.gst)} = ${nm(we4.amount)}$`], v: we4Bars, n: 'First the discount: fifteen percent of twenty four thousand is three thousand six hundred, so the selling price is twenty thousand four hundred rupees. Now the tax on that: eighteen percent is three thousand six hundred and seventy two rupees, which is one thousand eight hundred and thirty six of each kind. The customer pays twenty four thousand and seventy two rupees, just a little above the list price.' }),
    sl('diagram', 'A common mistake: taxing the list price', { b: r`Fifteen percent off and then $18\%$ GST is **not** "$3\%$ more". The multiplier is $0.85 \times 1.18 = 1.003$. Taxing the list price uses $1.18$ and overcharges.`, v: mistakePlot, n: 'Here is a mistake to avoid. Some students add the tax to the list price. The steep line shows that wrong method. The correct method multiplies the list price by nought point eight five and then by one point one eight, which is one point nought nought three. Also notice that fifteen off and eighteen on is not simply three percent more; the multiplier is only slightly above one.' }),
    sl('check', 'Quick check: half of eighteen', { b: 'For a sale within one state at a GST rate of 18%, what is the rate of CGST?', chk: 'fb1', n: 'Fill in the blank with a number. Remember how the rate is shared when the buyer and seller are in the same state.' }),
    sl('example', 'Worked example 5: one jacket, two bills', { b: `A woollen jacket is listed at ${R(we5.list)} with a discount of 15%. GST is 12%. Find the bill for a buyer in Srinagar when the seller is (i) in Srinagar; (ii) in Ludhiana (Punjab).`, ul: [r`Selling price $= 3600 - \dfrac{15}{100} \times 3600 = ${nm(we5.sp)}$; GST $= \dfrac{12}{100} \times ${nm(we5.sp)} = ${nm(we5.gst)}$`, r`(i) CGST $= \text{SGST} = ${nm(we5.cgst)}$; (ii) IGST $= ${nm(we5i.igst)}$`, `Amount ${R(we5.amount)} in both cases`], v: we5Table, n: 'The discount gives a selling price of three thousand and sixty rupees, and twelve percent of that is three hundred and sixty seven rupees twenty paise. If the seller is also in Srinagar, it splits into one hundred and eighty three rupees sixty paise of each kind. If the seller is in Ludhiana, the jacket crosses a state border and the whole amount is integrated tax. The buyer pays the same total.' }),
    sl('check', 'Quick check: a helmet in Vadodara', { b: 'A helmet set priced at ₹3,850 is sold within Vadodara at a GST rate of 28%. Find the CGST in rupees.', chk: 'n01', n: 'First decide the central rate from twenty eight percent. Then take that percentage of the price. Type the number of rupees.' }),
    sl('summary', 'Summary of Part 1', { ul: ['GST is a rate per cent of the price after discount', 'Within a state: CGST = SGST = half the GST', 'Between states: IGST at the full rate', 'Several items: tax each line at its own rate, then add', 'Discount first, then GST on the selling price'], v: summary1, n: 'To sum up part one. The tax is a percentage of the price after any discount. Within a state it splits equally into central and state tax. Between states it is charged whole as integrated tax. On a bill with several items, tax each line at its own rate. And with a discount, always reduce the price first and then add the tax.' }),
    sl('exam-tips', 'Exam tips for Part 1', { ul: ['Write "intra-state" or "inter-state" before you calculate', 'Show the GST, then the split: CGST and SGST each at **half the rate**', 'Never tax the list price when a discount is given', 'Keep paise: ₹158.50, not ₹158 or ₹159', 'State the final answer in rupees with a sentence'], n: 'Some tips for the examination. Before calculating, write whether the sale is within a state or between states; that decides the heads of tax. Show the full tax first and then the split, because each step carries a mark. Never charge tax on the list price when a discount is offered. Keep the paise exactly as they come. And finish with a clear sentence giving the answer in rupees.' }),
  ],
});

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- deck 2
const d2 = deck({
  part: 2,
  title: 'Goods and Services Tax — Part 2: Input tax credit, the supply chain and reverse problems',
  goals: ['Find the GST each dealer pays using input tax credit', 'Combine GST with profit, loss, marked price and discount', 'Show that the total tax to the government equals the tax paid by the consumer', 'Work backwards to find a price, a rate of GST, a list price or a rate of discount'],
  slides: [
    sl('title', 'Goods and Services Tax, Part 2: who pays what', { b: 'Tax is collected at every sale, yet the consumer bears it all.', v: chainPay, n: 'Welcome to part two. Goods usually pass through several hands before they reach you: a maker, a wholesaler, a retailer. Tax is charged at every sale. So who actually pays what? In this part you will see how each dealer pays only on its own value added, why the total equals the tax on the last sale, and how to work backwards from a bill.' }),
    sl('concept', 'What you will be able to do', { ul: ['Use **input tax credit** to find each dealer\'s **net GST**', 'Split each payment into **CGST** and **SGST**, or **IGST**', 'Combine GST with **profit, loss, marked price and discount**', 'Find a **price, rate, list price or discount** from the amount paid'], n: 'Here are the goals for part two. You will use input tax credit to find the net tax each dealer pays, and split it into central and state parts, or integrated tax across states. You will mix the tax with profit, loss and discounts. And you will solve reverse problems, finding a price or a rate from the amount paid.' }),
    sl('concept', 'Input tax, output tax and input tax credit', { b: 'A dealer **pays** GST when it buys (input tax) and **collects** GST when it sells (output tax). It hands over only the difference.', m: [r`\text{net GST payable} = \text{output tax} - \text{input tax}`], ul: ['The deduction of input tax is called **input tax credit (ITC)**', 'Within a state, CGST credit is used against CGST and SGST credit against SGST'], v: itcFlow, n: 'When a dealer buys stock, it pays tax to its supplier. This is called input tax. When it sells, it collects tax from its customer. This is called output tax. The dealer does not hand over the whole output tax; it subtracts the input tax it has already paid. This deduction is called input tax credit, and the dealer pays only the difference.' }),
    sl('diagram', 'Why input tax credit matters', { b: 'Without the credit, tax would be charged on tax at every stage.', v: cascade, n: 'Imagine there were no credit. Every dealer would pay tax on its full selling price, and the price at each stage would already include the earlier taxes. In this chain the total would be one thousand nine hundred and forty four rupees instead of seven hundred and fifty six. This piling up is called a cascade. Input tax credit removes it, so each stage pays only on the value it adds.' }),
    sl('example', 'Worked example 1: a chain within Gujarat', { b: `A mixer-grinder is sold by a maker in Surat to a wholesaler in Vadodara for ${R(rs(3000))}, then to a retailer in Rajkot for ${R(rs(3600))}, then to a customer in Rajkot for ${R(rs(4200))}. GST is 18%. Find the GST each dealer pays, as CGST and SGST.`, ul: [r`Maker: output $540$, no input: pays ${R(c1.stages[0].pay)}`, r`Wholesaler: $648 - 540 = ${nm(c1.stages[1].pay)}$; retailer: $756 - 648 = ${nm(c1.stages[2].pay)}$`, `Each payment splits equally: ${R(c1.stages[0].cgstPay)}, ${R(c1.stages[1].cgstPay)}, ${R(c1.stages[2].cgstPay)} of CGST`], v: c1Table, n: 'Work stage by stage. The maker has no input tax, so it pays the full eighteen percent of three thousand, five hundred and forty rupees. The wholesaler collects six hundred and forty eight and has already paid five hundred and forty, so it pays one hundred and eight. The retailer collects seven hundred and fifty six and pays one hundred and eight. Every payment splits into equal central and state halves.' }),
    sl('diagram', 'The payments add up to the consumer\'s tax', { b: r`$540 + 108 + 108 = 756 = 18\% \text{ of } 4200$: the government receives exactly the tax on the last sale.`, v: payBars, n: 'Now add the three payments: five hundred and forty, one hundred and eight and one hundred and eight make seven hundred and fifty six rupees. That is exactly eighteen percent of the final price of four thousand two hundred, the tax the customer pays. The dealers only collect and pass on tax; the consumer bears all of it.' }),
    sl('check', 'Quick check: net GST', { b: 'A dealer collected ₹1,440 of GST on sales and had paid ₹1,080 of GST on purchases of the same goods. How much does the dealer pay the government?', chk: 'm10', n: 'Which amount is the output tax and which is the input tax? Decide what the dealer actually hands over, then choose.' }),
    sl('concept', 'A shortcut for one rate', { b: 'When the rate is the same on buying and selling:', m: [r`\text{net GST} = \dfrac{r}{100} \times \text{SP} - \dfrac{r}{100} \times \text{CP} = \dfrac{r}{100} \times (\text{SP} - \text{CP})`], ul: ['The dealer pays GST only on its **margin** (value added)', 'Profit or loss is worked out on prices **before** tax: GST is not the dealer\'s money', 'At a loss the net figure is negative: nothing to pay, the rest stays as credit'], v: cascade, n: 'If the rate is the same when the dealer buys and sells, the net tax is the rate times the selling price minus the rate times the cost price, which is the rate times the margin. So a dealer pays tax only on the value it adds. Profit and loss are always worked out on prices before tax, because the tax is not the dealer’s own money. At a loss, nothing is payable.' }),
    sl('example', 'Worked example 2: marked price, discount and GST', { b: `A music shop in Kolkata buys a harmonium for ${R(d2cost)} (before GST at 12%). It marks the harmonium 20% above cost, gives a local customer 10% off and charges GST at 12%. Find the GST the shop pays, as CGST and SGST, the amount the customer pays and the shop's profit percent.`, ul: [r`Marked price $= 5000 \times 1.2 = ${nm(d2mp)}$; selling price $= ${nm(d2mp)} \times 0.9 = ${nm(d2sp)}$`, r`Output $= ${nm(w2.out.gst)}$, input $= ${nm(w2.input.gst)}$, net $= ${nm(w2.pay)}$: CGST ${R(w2.cgstPay)} and SGST ${R(w2.sgstPay)}`, r`Customer pays ${R(w2.out.amount)}; profit $= \dfrac{400}{5000} \times 100 = ${profitPct(d2cost, d2sp)}\%$`], v: we2Flow, n: 'Mark up first: twenty percent above five thousand is six thousand. Ten percent off gives a selling price of five thousand four hundred. The shop collects twelve percent of that, six hundred and forty eight rupees, and has already paid six hundred, so it pays forty eight: twenty four of each kind. The customer pays six thousand and forty eight, and the shop’s profit is eight percent.' }),
    sl('concept', 'When a link crosses a state border', { b: 'On an **inter-state** sale the dealer charges **IGST**. Its input CGST and SGST are both set off against that IGST.', m: [r`\text{IGST payable} = \text{output IGST} - (\text{input CGST} + \text{input SGST})`], v: interTable, n: 'Sometimes one link of the chain crosses a state border. Then the seller charges integrated tax on that sale. Any central and state tax it paid on its purchase can be set off against this integrated tax, so it pays only the difference, as integrated tax. The table shows an example from Punjab to Rajasthan.' }),
    sl('example', 'Worked example 3: Ludhiana to Jaipur', { b: `A blanket maker in Ludhiana sells a lot to a distributor in Ludhiana for ${R(rs(40000))}. The distributor sells it to a retailer in Jaipur (Rajasthan) for ${R(rs(46000))}. GST is 12%. Find the tax the distributor pays to the government.`, ul: [r`Purchase (intra-state): CGST $= \text{SGST} = 6\% \text{ of } 40000 = ${nm(c3m.out.cgst)}$; input tax ${R(c3d.input.gst)}`, r`Sale (inter-state): IGST $= \dfrac{12}{100} \times 46000 = ${nm(c3d.out.igst)}$`, r`IGST payable $= ${nm(c3d.out.igst)} - ${nm(c3d.input.gst)} = ${nm(c3d.igstPay)}$`], v: interTable, n: 'The distributor’s purchase is inside Punjab, so it pays central and state tax of two thousand four hundred rupees each, four thousand eight hundred in all. Its sale goes to Rajasthan, so it charges integrated tax of twelve percent on forty six thousand, five thousand five hundred and twenty rupees. Setting off the input tax, it pays seven hundred and twenty rupees of integrated tax.' }),
    sl('check', 'Quick check: tax on the margin', { b: 'True or false: when the GST rate is the same on its purchase and its sale, a retailer\'s net GST payable equals that rate applied to its margin (selling price minus purchase price).', chk: 'tf3', n: 'Think about the shortcut you have just seen. Does it hold for any selling price above the cost price? Decide, then answer.' }),
    sl('concept', 'Working backwards', { b: 'In a **reverse problem** you know the amount paid and must find what came before it. Undo the steps in **reverse order**: the tax first, then the discount.', v: reverseTable, n: 'Some problems give you the final amount and ask for something earlier: the price before tax, the rate, the list price or the discount. Undo the steps in reverse order. The tax was added last, so remove it first, by dividing by one plus the rate over one hundred. Then undo the discount. The table lists the rules; each one comes from writing an equation and solving it.' }),
    sl('example', 'Worked example 4: the price inside an amount', { b: `A family in Mysuru pays ${R(rs(11092))} for a washing machine, including GST at 18%. Find the price before GST and the CGST.`, ul: [r`Let the price be ₹$x$: $x + \dfrac{18}{100}x = 11092$, so $1.18x = 11092$`, r`$x = \dfrac{11092}{1.18} = ${nm(we24)}$`, r`CGST $= 9\% \text{ of } ${nm(we24)} = ${nm(we24s.cgst)}$`], v: inclPlot, n: 'Call the price x. The amount is x plus eighteen percent of x, which is one point one eight x. So one point one eight x equals eleven thousand and ninety two, and x is nine thousand four hundred rupees. Do not take eighteen percent off the amount; that gives the wrong answer because the tax was added to the smaller number. The central tax is nine percent of the price, eight hundred and forty six rupees.' }),
    sl('example', 'Worked example 5: finding the rate', { b: `A ceiling lamp listed at ${R(rs(2500))} is sold at a discount of 8%, and the customer pays ${R(rs(2576))}. Find the rate of GST.`, ul: ['Price after discount: $2500 - 200 = 2300$', 'GST: $2576 - 2300 = 276$', r`Rate $= \dfrac{276}{2300} \times 100 = ${we25g}\%$`], v: we25Table, n: 'First find the taxable value: eight percent off two thousand five hundred leaves two thousand three hundred rupees. The tax is the amount paid minus this, two hundred and seventy six rupees. The rate is the tax as a percentage of the price before tax: two hundred and seventy six over two thousand three hundred, times one hundred, which is twelve percent.' }),
    sl('check', 'Quick check: undo the tax', { b: 'A customer pays ₹3,360 for a mattress, including GST at 12%. Find the price before GST.', chk: 'fb5', n: 'Undo the tax: what number, increased by twelve percent, gives three thousand three hundred and sixty? Type the price in rupees.' }),
    sl('example', 'Worked example 6: finding the list price', { b: `After a discount of 16% on the list price and GST at 18% on the reduced price, a customer pays ${R(rs(4956))} for a cupboard. Find the list price.`, ul: [r`Selling price $= \dfrac{4956}{1.18} = ${nm(we26sp)}$`, r`List price $= \dfrac{${nm(we26sp)}}{0.84} = ${nm(we26l)}$`, r`Check: $5000 \times 0.84 \times 1.18 = 4956$`], v: we26Flow, n: 'Undo the last step first. Dividing the amount by one point one eight gives the selling price, four thousand two hundred rupees. The discount left eighty four percent of the list price, so divide by nought point eight four to get the list price, five thousand rupees. Always check by working forwards again: five thousand, times nought point eight four, times one point one eight, is four thousand nine hundred and fifty six.' }),
    sl('check', 'Quick check: a retailer in Mangaluru', { b: 'A retailer buys a gas stove for ₹6,400 and sells it within the state for ₹7,200 (both before tax). GST is 18%. Find the CGST the retailer pays.', chk: 'n05', n: 'Use the shortcut: the retailer pays tax only on its margin. Then take the central half of that tax.' }),
    sl('summary', 'Summary of Part 2', { ul: ['Net GST = output tax − input tax (input tax credit)', 'At one rate: net GST = rate × (selling price − purchase price)', 'Total GST to the government = GST on the final sale, borne by the consumer', 'Inter-state link: IGST payable = output IGST − input CGST − input SGST', 'Reverse problems: undo the tax, then the discount'], v: summary2, n: 'To sum up part two. Each dealer pays output tax minus input tax. At one rate, that is the rate times its margin. Added along the chain, the payments equal the tax on the final sale, which the consumer bears. On an inter-state link, the dealer pays integrated tax after setting off its input tax. In reverse problems, undo the tax first and then the discount.' }),
    sl('exam-tips', 'Exam tips for Part 2', { ul: ['Make a table: stage, selling price, output tax, input tax, net GST', 'The first seller in the chain has **no input tax**', 'Profit and loss are on prices **before** tax', 'In reverse problems divide by $1.18$ (or $1 + r/100$); never take $r\\%$ off the amount', 'Check a reverse answer by working forwards'], n: 'For long questions on a chain, draw a table with the selling price, output tax, input tax and net tax at each stage; examiners give marks for each column. Remember the first seller has no input tax. Work out profit and loss on prices before tax. In reverse problems divide by one plus the rate, never take the rate off the amount. And check by working forwards.' }),
  ],
});

// ---------------------------------------------------------------- notes
const notes = {
  unit: 'icse-10-mathematics-ch01', version: 1, title: 'Goods and Services Tax (GST) — chapter notes', language: 'en',
  sections: [
    { heading: '1. What GST is', body_md: 'The **Goods and Services Tax (GST)** is a single tax on the supply of goods and services in India, in force since 1 July 2017. It replaced many earlier indirect taxes (central excise, VAT, service tax and others).\n\n- It is charged at **every sale** in a supply chain: manufacturer → wholesaler → retailer → consumer.\n- It applies to **services** as well as goods (repairs, catering, rent, software).\n- The **buyer pays** it, the **seller collects** it and passes it to the government.\n- Rates are fixed for each item by the **GST Council** and are revised from time to time. In every problem the rate is **given**; this chapter uses 5%, 12%, 18% and 28%.', key_terms: [{ term: 'GST', definition_md: 'Goods and Services Tax: one tax on the supply of goods and services across India.' }, { term: 'Taxable value', definition_md: 'The price on which GST is charged: the selling price after any discount, before tax.' }, { term: 'GST Council', definition_md: 'The body of the Union and state finance ministers that fixes and revises GST rates.' }], formulas: [{ latex: r`\text{GST} = \dfrac{r}{100} \times \text{taxable value}`, label: 'GST at rate r%' }], visual: chainFlow, topic_ids: [topic(1)] },
    { heading: '2. CGST, SGST and IGST', body_md: 'Where the seller and the buyer are decides the **heads of tax**:\n\n- **Intra-state** sale (same state): the bill shows **CGST** (central) and **SGST** (state), each at **half** the GST rate.\n- **Inter-state** sale (different states): the bill shows **IGST** (integrated) at the **full** rate. The Centre collects it and shares it with the state where the goods are used.\n\nThe rate and the total tax are the same in both cases; only the heads differ. (In a union territory without its own legislature, UTGST takes the place of SGST.)\n\nExample: a pump priced ₹15,500 at 18%: Coimbatore to Palakkad, IGST ₹2,790; Coimbatore to Tiruppur, CGST ₹1,395 + SGST ₹1,395.', key_terms: [{ term: 'CGST', definition_md: 'Central GST on an intra-state sale, at half the GST rate.' }, { term: 'SGST', definition_md: 'State GST on an intra-state sale, at half the GST rate.' }, { term: 'IGST', definition_md: 'Integrated GST on an inter-state sale, at the full rate.' }], formulas: [{ latex: r`\text{CGST} = \text{SGST} = \dfrac{1}{2}\,\text{GST}`, label: 'Intra-state' }, { latex: r`\text{IGST} = \text{GST}`, label: 'Inter-state' }], visual: headsTable, visuals: [rateTable], topic_ids: [topic(1)] },
    { heading: '3. The tax invoice', body_md: `A registered seller issues a **tax invoice** showing the taxable value of each item, the rate and amount of CGST and SGST (or IGST), and the invoice value.\n\nWith items at **different rates**, tax each line at its own rate, then add:\n\n- School bag ₹1,200 at 12%: GST ₹144\n- Calculator ₹850 at 18%: GST ₹153\n- Notebooks ₹400 at 5%: GST ₹20\n\nTotal GST ${R(we3.gst)}; CGST = SGST = ${R(we3.cgst)}; invoice value ${R(we3.amount)}.`, key_terms: [{ term: 'Tax invoice', definition_md: 'The bill a registered seller issues, showing taxable value, each tax and the total.' }, { term: 'Invoice value', definition_md: 'Taxable value plus all the taxes: the amount the buyer pays.' }], formulas: [{ latex: r`\text{invoice value} = \text{taxable value} + \text{CGST} + \text{SGST} \ (\text{or} + \text{IGST})`, label: 'Bill' }], visual: we3Table, topic_ids: [topic(1)] },
    { heading: '4. Discount and GST: what the consumer pays', body_md: `GST is charged on the **selling price after discount**, never on the list price.\n\n1. Discount = d% of the list price; selling price = list price − discount.\n2. GST = r% of the selling price.\n3. Amount paid = selling price + GST = list price × (1 − d/100) × (1 + r/100).\n\nExample: list price ${R(we4.list)}, 15% off, GST 18%: selling price ${R(we4.sp)}, GST ${R(we4.gst)}, amount ${R(we4.amount)}. Note that 15% off and 18% on multiply to 1.003, not 1.03.`, key_terms: [{ term: 'List (marked) price', definition_md: 'The price printed or quoted before any discount.' }, { term: 'Selling price', definition_md: 'The list price minus the discount; it is the taxable value.' }], formulas: [{ latex: r`\text{amount} = L\left(1 - \dfrac{d}{100}\right)\left(1 + \dfrac{r}{100}\right)`, label: 'List price L, discount d%, GST r%' }], visual: discFlow, visuals: [mistakePlot], topic_ids: [topic(2)] },
    { heading: '5. Input tax credit and GST with profit or loss', body_md: `A dealer **pays** GST on purchases (**input tax**) and **collects** GST on sales (**output tax**). Through **input tax credit** it pays only the difference:\n\n$$\\text{net GST} = \\text{output tax} - \\text{input tax}.$$\n\n- Within a state, CGST payable = SGST payable = half the net GST.\n- At one rate, net GST = r% of (selling price − purchase price): tax on the **value added**.\n- **Profit and loss** are worked out on prices **before** tax; the tax is passed on, not earned.\n- At a **loss**, output tax < input tax: nothing is payable and the difference stays as credit.\n\nExample: cost ${R(d2cost)}, marked 20% up, 10% off, GST 12%: selling price ${R(d2sp)}, net GST ${R(w2.pay)} (CGST ${R(w2.cgstPay)} + SGST ${R(w2.sgstPay)}), customer pays ${R(w2.out.amount)}, profit ${profitPct(d2cost, d2sp)}%.`, key_terms: [{ term: 'Input tax', definition_md: 'GST a dealer pays when it buys goods or services for its business.' }, { term: 'Output tax', definition_md: 'GST a dealer charges and collects when it sells.' }, { term: 'Input tax credit (ITC)', definition_md: 'Setting off input tax against output tax, so that only the difference is paid.' }], formulas: [{ latex: r`\text{net GST} = \text{output tax} - \text{input tax}`, label: 'Tax payable by a dealer' }, { latex: r`\text{net GST} = \dfrac{r}{100}(\text{SP} - \text{CP})`, label: 'One rate throughout' }], visual: itcFlow, visuals: [we2Flow], topic_ids: [topic(3)] },
    { heading: '6. The supply chain: tax at each stage', body_md: `Make a table: stage, selling price, output tax, input tax, net GST (and CGST, SGST).\n\n- The **first seller** has no input tax and pays the full GST on its price.\n- Each later dealer pays output tax minus the previous stage's output tax.\n- Adding the payments, the intermediate taxes cancel: **total GST to the government = GST on the final sale**, which the **consumer** bears.\n\nExample (18%, all in Gujarat): sales at ₹3,000, ₹3,600, ₹4,200; payments ₹540, ₹108, ₹108; total ${R(c1.total)} = 18% of ₹4,200. Without input tax credit the taxes would total ${R(noItc)} (tax on tax).\n\n**Inter-state link:** the seller charges IGST and sets off its input CGST and SGST: IGST payable = output IGST − input CGST − input SGST. Example: ${R(c3d.out.igst)} − ${R(c3d.input.gst)} = ${R(c3d.igstPay)}.`, key_terms: [{ term: 'Supply chain', definition_md: 'The sequence of sales from manufacturer to wholesaler to retailer to consumer.' }, { term: 'Cascading', definition_md: 'Tax charged on a price that already includes tax; input tax credit prevents it.' }], formulas: [{ latex: r`\text{total GST} = \dfrac{r}{100} \times \text{final selling price}`, label: 'Whole chain, one rate' }], visual: c1Table, visuals: [payBars, interTable], topic_ids: [topic(3)] },
    { heading: '7. Reverse problems', body_md: `Undo the steps in **reverse order**: the tax was added last, so remove it first.\n\n- Price before GST: price = amount × 100/(100 + r). Example: ${R(rs(11092))} at 18% → ${R(we24)}.\n- Rate of GST: r = (amount − price)/price × 100. Example: ${R(we25sp)} → ${R(rs(2576))} gives ${we25g}%.\n- List price: list = selling price ÷ (1 − d/100). Example: ${R(rs(4956))} after 16% off and 18% GST → selling price ${R(we26sp)} → list ${R(we26l)}.\n- Rate of discount: d = (list − selling price)/list × 100.\n- From a dealer's payment at one rate: its margin = payment × 100/r.\n\nNever take r% **off** the amount: 82% of ₹1,180 is ₹967.60, but the price is ₹1,000.`, key_terms: [{ term: 'Reverse (inverse) problem', definition_md: 'A problem that gives the final amount and asks for an earlier quantity: price, rate, list price or discount.' }], formulas: [{ latex: r`\text{price} = \text{amount} \times \dfrac{100}{100 + r}`, label: 'Price before GST' }, { latex: r`r = \dfrac{\text{amount} - \text{price}}{\text{price}} \times 100`, label: 'Rate of GST' }], visual: reverseTable, visuals: [inclPlot], topic_ids: [topic(4)] },
  ],
  exam_tips_md: '**How marks are awarded**\n- *GST on a bill (2–3 marks):* GST on each item (1), CGST and SGST or IGST correctly chosen and split (1), invoice value (1).\n- *Discount and GST (3 marks):* selling price after discount (1), GST on the selling price (1), amount paid (1).\n- *Supply chain (4 marks in Section A, or a 3 + 3 + 4 structured question):* selling price at each stage (1), output and input tax (1), net GST of each dealer (1), CGST/SGST split or the total equal to the consumer\'s tax (1).\n- *Reverse problems (3–4 marks):* equation such as $1.18x = \\text{amount}$ (1), price (1), then rate, list price or discount (1–2).\n\n**Common mistakes**\n1. Charging GST on the list price instead of the price after discount.\n2. Using IGST for a sale within a state, or CGST and SGST for a sale between states.\n3. Writing CGST at the full rate instead of half.\n4. Forgetting that the first seller in a chain has no input tax.\n5. Adding the output taxes of all stages to get the government\'s total.\n6. Working out profit on prices that include GST.\n7. Taking r% off the amount paid to remove the tax (divide by 1 + r/100 instead).\n8. Rounding paise: ₹158.50 is exact, not ₹159.',
  status: 'draft', provenance: d1.provenance,
};
must(pctOf(rs(1180), 82) === rs(967.6) && baseFrom(rs(1180), 18) === rs(1000), 'notes 7 example');

